{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising markets: when the last 5–10 days show an upswing (positive slope), add an upward level shift to the whole path. In these samples the model is ~2–6 EUR too low on average, with the bias increasing with horizon.",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: apply a correction that grows with lead time (small at days 1–3, larger by days 10–30). The model’s error is modest early but becomes large in weeks 2–4 as it fails to follow the sustained rally.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: when price breaks above the recent 30d range or posts consecutive higher highs, increase forecast drift (daily increments) rather than keeping a flat/mean-reverting profile; the model reacts too slowly to regime shifts.",
    "Over-mean-reversion: reduce pullback strength after dips during an emerging uptrend. The model repeatedly projects returns toward ~62–65 while realized prices re-rate to ~67–70.",
    "Volatility compression: widen forecast amplitude after a breakout. The model’s path is too smooth and misses the magnitude of the move; scale deviations from the starting level upward (especially mid/late horizon) when recent realized volatility rises."
  ]
}
